# 2026-07-30, X-15: worker.zig 657 -> 722, RAISED DELIBERATELY AND EXPECTED TO FALL.
#
#   A worker that could not initialise used to log and return from inside its own
#   thread, which is the only thing a thread entry point can do. The pool had
#   already reported success, so the daemon ran with fewer workers than it claimed
#   -- or none -- and, worse, that worker's quiescent-state slot was never written
#   again. Because the safe generation is the MINIMUM across slots, one frozen slot
#   pins reclamation for the whole process: every SIGHUP then retires a
#   configuration that can never be freed. An unbounded leak on a daemon that
#   looks healthy. See rcu.zig's test "a worker that never quiesces pins
#   reclamation permanently", which measures exactly that.
#
#   The fix is a RELOCATION, not a barrier: every worker is now constructed on the
#   calling thread, where `try` still works, and threads are spawned only once all
#   of them exist. No cross-thread signalling was added. What cost the 65 lines is
#   the ownership bookkeeping that relocation makes explicit -- which worker owns
#   its wakeup pipe read end, and which are still ours to tear down on each error
#   path. That was previously "free" only because a leaked fd inside a dying thread
#   is invisible.
#
#   THIS RAISE SHOULD NOT SURVIVE. There is a real seam here -- worker.zig is one
#   worker's event loop, and pool construction/ownership is a separate concern, the
#   same split as daemon.zig vs bootstrap.zig. It is deliberately NOT done in this
#   commit: a finding fix and a structural refactor should be reviewable apart. The
#   next commit moves the pool out and brings this back under 657.
#
#   PAID, 2026-07-30. `pool.zig` now holds spawnPool, spawnPoolWithReload, the thread
#   entry point and the two X-15 tests; worker.zig is 722 -> 601, which is under both
#   657 and the 659 from stage 3.1 above. Nothing changed in the move -- the ownership
#   rules were written against a measured leak, so they were transcribed rather than
#   rewritten, and the tests moved with the code they test. Callers name the seam:
#   `securemilter.pool.spawnPoolWithReload`, inline rather than aliased, because one
#   call site does not earn a module alias and an alias would have cost each daemon a
#   line for nothing.
#
#   Worth keeping as a precedent: the raise was recorded WITH the obligation to undo
#   it and a number to hit. That is the only reason it got undone rather than becoming
#   the new normal.
#
